.1125,"pass_rate":0.6129,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5741,"def_epa_per_play":0.01168},{"week":17,"opponent":"BUF","home":false,"pf":26.0,"pa":56.0,"off_snaps":81,"epa_per_play":-0.1676,"pass_rate":0.7531,"pass_rate_neutral":0.625,"def_epa_per_play":0.2525}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":1.026,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"},{"season":2018,"team":"CHI","distance":1.072,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Matt Nagy"},{"season":2024,"team":"NO","distance":1.085,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Klint Kubiak"},{"season":2025,"team":"SF","distance":1.09,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Kyle Shanahan"},{"season":2019,"team":"DEN","distance":1.091,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2020,"team":"BAL","distance":0.7227,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"NE","distance":0.7941,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brian Flores"},{"season":2023,"team":"NYG","distance":0.8006,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NE","distance":0.8032,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"NYG","distance":0.8834,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Dolphins went 10-6 in 2020 and outscored opponents 404 to 338. The defense did most of the work, finishing 7th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 19th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd lowest 11 personnel usage and the highest Cover 0 rate in the league.","offense":"The Dolphins offense was a little below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd lowest 11 personnel usage and the 3rd highest 21 personnel usage in the league. Tua Tagovailoa ran the passing game, with DeVante Parker leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Dolphins defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 4th and 19th. Emmanuel Ogbah led the team with 9 sacks, and Jerome Baker added 7, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Schematically, they had the highest Cover 0 rate in the league.","special":"The Dolphins were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 19% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 92% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 27% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the youngest in the league.","players":"Tua Tagovailoa played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,814 yards. Targets were spread around, with DeVante Parker and Mike Gesicki leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Myles Gaskin and Salvon Ahmed. On defense, Emmanuel Ogbah led the team with 9 sacks and Xavien Howard picked off 10 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2022 49ers under Kyle Shanahan and the 2018 Bears under Matt Nagy. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Ravens and the 2018 Patriots under Brian Flores.","trends":"The offense went from 28th in EPA per play in 2019 to 19th in 2020, a big step forward. The defense moved from 32nd in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 7th in 2020, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was eight man box rate, which went from 4% to 17%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}